## Exploring small cases

- $f_0(x)=1$.
- $f_1'(x)=f_0(x+1)=1$, so $f_1(x)=x$ (using $f_1(0)=0$).
- $f_2'(x)=2f_1(x+1)=2(x+1)$, so $f_2(x)=(x+1)^2+C$; $f_2(0)=0\Rightarrow C=-1$, giving $f_2(x)=(x+1)^2-1=x(x+2)$.
- $f_3'(x)=3f_2(x+1)=3[(x+2)^2-1]=3(x+2)^2-3$, so $f_3(x)=(x+2)^3-3x+C$; $f_3(0)=0\Rightarrow C=-8$, giving $f_3(x)=x^3+6x^2+9x=x(x+3)^2$.

The pattern $f_1(x)=x,\ f_2(x)=x(x+2),\ f_3(x)=x(x+3)^2$ suggests

$$f_n(x)=x(x+n)^{n-1}\quad(n\ge 1).$$

## Proof by induction

**Base case** $n=1$: $f_1(x)=x=x(x+1)^0$. ✓

**Inductive step.** Suppose $f_n(x)=x(x+n)^{n-1}$. Then
$$f_{n+1}'(x)=(n+1)f_n(x+1)=(n+1)(x+1)(x+n+1)^{n-1}.$$
Consider the candidate $g(x)=x(x+n+1)^n$. Its derivative is
$$g'(x)=(x+n+1)^n+nx(x+n+1)^{n-1}=(x+n+1)^{n-1}\big[(x+n+1)+nx\big]$$
$$=(x+n+1)^{n-1}\big[(n+1)x+(n+1)\big]=(n+1)(x+1)(x+n+1)^{n-1}.$$
So $f_{n+1}'(x)=g'(x)$, hence $f_{n+1}(x)=g(x)+C$. Since $f_{n+1}(0)=0$ and $g(0)=0$, we get $C=0$, i.e.
$$f_{n+1}(x)=x(x+n+1)^n.$$
This completes the induction. ✓

## Computing $f_{100}(1)$

Setting $n=100$ and $x=1$:
$$f_{100}(1)=1\cdot(1+100)^{99}=101^{99}.$$

## Primality of 101

Since $\sqrt{101}<11$, it suffices to check divisibility by $2,3,5,7$:
- $101$ is odd,
- $1+0+1=2$ is not divisible by $3$,
- $101$ does not end in $0$ or $5$,
- $7\cdot 14=98,\ 7\cdot 15=105$, so $7\nmid 101$.

Hence $101$ is prime.

## Conclusion

$$\boxed{f_{100}(1)=101^{99}}.$$

The explicit factorization of $f_{100}(1)$ into powers of distinct primes is $\mathbf{101^{99}}$.

